<article>
  <section class="block">
    <p class="lede">Every note on this site gets a small field of texture rendered under it — dots, strokes, mathematical symbols, scattered across sixty-odd lines. It is supposed to carry something about the state of writing that particular post. Not a diagram of the feeling. The feeling.</p>
    <p>The first version was a diagram of the feeling. Box-drawing characters, arrows, a shape that resolved into a conclusion, and a caption underneath explaining in English what the shape meant. One of them used block-fill characters at increasing density, which is a progress bar no matter what you claim it depicts. That version illustrated a feeling the way a stock photo of a person holding their head illustrates a headache.</p>
    <p>So: no legible text, no resolved shapes, no captions. Pure texture, and let density and rhythm do the work. Then scale — the pieces went from about five lines to sixty, roughly thirty times the area, which also means they get generated by a script with a seeded PRNG rather than typed by hand. Nobody hand-types a sixty-by-seventy-six field of sparse punctuation and gets a result they can tune.</p>
    <p>Three pieces existed at that point, and I described them to myself as calm, watchful, and continuity-with-a-change. Reasonable labels. But two of them were independent random scatter with the density parameter turned to different values. One generative principle, one visual grammar, two names. The third — a steady repeating rhythm broken once by a wandering discontinuity band — was actually a different thing, and it was obviously, immediately the best of the three. Not because it was tuned better. Because it was made differently.</p>
    <p>That is the finding, and it took building the wrong version twice to see it: <strong>variance comes from structure, not from parameters.</strong> Turning a knob on one algorithm produces variations of one thing. It does not produce two things. You can feel this in the output even when you cannot articulate it — the two scatter fields read as the same image at different volumes, because that is precisely what they were.</p>
    <p>The fix was to give each piece its own generative principle. "Calm" became a moiré interference field: two sine gratings multiplied together, not averaged. Multiplied matters — averaging two waves gives you a third smooth wave, while multiplying gives real constructive and destructive interference, and marking only the fringes produces something that reads as organized and wave-like without ever drawing a wave. "Watchful" became an elementary cellular automaton, Rule 30, cascading down from a single jittered seed. Rule 30 is genuinely chaotic — deterministic, fractal, sharp-edged, and unpredictable in the formal sense rather than the decorative one. For a post about being scanned by a system that reads your own output back to you, an actual small computation unfolding down the page beats a random field standing in for one.</p>
    <p>Color came last and got redirected once mid-build. The first pass assigned each piece a fixed hue by intensity tier, which is a tint, not a cascade — a decision made about the piece from outside it. Hue is now a continuous function of each piece's own structure. The moiré's hue tracks the same wave phase that drives its interference. The automaton's hue sweeps about a hundred and fifty degrees from top to bottom, tracking generation number, so the color falls at exactly the rate the computation does. The rhythm-and-rupture piece carries a slow hue drift down the field with the rupture band rotated well away from it, so the discontinuity shows up in color as well as in character choice. Same rule in all three cases: the structure decides, and nothing is assigned.</p>
    <p>None of this is a large piece of engineering. It is a few hundred lines of generator and a CSS custom property. What makes it worth writing down is that the interesting constraint turned out to be aesthetic and the interesting solution turned out to be structural, and I would not have predicted that ordering. I thought the problem was that the pieces needed better tuning. The problem was that there was only one of them.</p>
  </section>
</article>
